// --- EXPERIMENT, not part of the shipped fix: halve ALL root motion ---
// Movement in BT3 is 100% root motion through the skeleton. With exactly one
// writer each way (both confirmed by write breakpoint), the per-frame cycle is
//   model+0x950  = fighter+0x10 + fighter+0x30     FUN_001D7198, 001D71F4
//   fighter+0x10 = model+0x970 - fighter+0x30      FUN_001D70E8, 001D7118
// which reduces to  fighter+0x10 += (model+0x970 - model+0x950).  So the whole
// per-frame displacement is that difference, produced by the skeleton pass.
// Replacing model+0x970 with the midpoint of the two halves it exactly.
// EXPECTED: airborne motion becomes correct, GROUND motion becomes half speed.
// That asymmetry is the point of the test - it proves both that this is the one
// movement channel and that ground is already compensated while air is not.
// The scripted-sequence clock, found 2026-09-07 - the one behind everything the
// game stages rather than simulates. FUN_00158850 steps one node of a scripted
// sequence per tick, and a step that is waiting counts a plain integer down by
// one at 00158914 ([node+4]) and 0015894C ([node+8]). Both waits are authored in
// 30Hz frames, so at 60fps every scripted beat - a camera cut, a mouth line, a
// fade, the moment an ultimate lets go of its beam - arrives in half its real
// time. Counting down on even ticks only restores all of them at once, and
// unlike gating the node's update it skips nothing: the step still runs, still
// spawns, still draws, it just waits the right length of time.
[60FPS - sequence wait]
description=Count scripted-sequence waits down at 30Hz, so staged beats keep their real timing.
patch=1,EE,000F0AC0,word,8E020004 // lw $v0, 4($s0)        the wait counter, reloaded
patch=1,EE,000F0AC4,word,3C010033 // lui $at, 0x33
patch=1,EE,000F0AC8,word,8C211D64 // lw $at, 0x1d64($at)   global frame counter
patch=1,EE,000F0ACC,word,30210001 // andi $at, $at, 1
patch=1,EE,000F0AD0,word,14200002 // bnez $at, 000F0ADC    odd tick: hold
patch=1,EE,000F0AD4,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F0AD8,word,2442FFFF // addiu $v0, $v0, -1    even tick: count down
patch=1,EE,000F0ADC,word,08056246 // j 0x158918
patch=1,EE,000F0AE0,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,00158910,word,0803C2B0 // lw -> j 000F0AC0
patch=1,EE,000F0AE4,word,8E020008 // lw $v0, 8($s0)        the second wait, same shape
patch=1,EE,000F0AE8,word,3C010033 // lui $at, 0x33
patch=1,EE,000F0AEC,word,8C211D64 // lw $at, 0x1d64($at)
patch=1,EE,000F0AF0,word,30210001 // andi $at, $at, 1
patch=1,EE,000F0AF4,word,14200002 // bnez $at, 000F0B00
patch=1,EE,000F0AF8,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F0AFC,word,2442FFFF // addiu $v0, $v0, -1
patch=1,EE,000F0B00,word,08056254 // j 0x158950
patch=1,EE,000F0B04,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,00158948,word,0803C2B9 // lw -> j 000F0AE4

// The fighter state machine's phase timers, found 2026-09-07 - the ones the
// player feels most. Every attack, charge, block and recovery is a state whose
// handler keeps a counter in the scratch block the dispatcher zeroes on entry
// (FUN_001E23D0 memsets fighter+0x3D0), advances it once a tick, and compares it
// against a count authored in 30Hz frames: how long a Blast 2 charges before it
// lets go, how long a recovery lasts, when a beam is released. At 60fps all 28
// of them expire in half their real time.
//
// An integer cannot be halved, and freezing one hangs the state, so each site
// jumps to a trampoline that adds one on even ticks only. Nothing else changes -
// the store in the branch's delay slot still runs every tick, so any code
// reading the timer as a level rather than a clock still sees it every frame.
// Six of the 28 are left alone: 001F1C74, 001F31C0, 001FBF28, 001FCE34,
// 001FF9A8 and 001FFC10. Those counters are levels rather than clocks - a
// combo index, an input window - and gating them costs an ordinary rush its
// fourth hit or cuts the charge short. They were picked out by measurement,
// one site at a time, against a held Blast 2 and a mashed rush.
// Generated by tools/phasetimer.py | tools/mkgate.py.
[60FPS - airborne motion]
description=Halve both the displacement and the ramp of the airborne directed-travel channel (FUN_001DE000): flight, dashes and knockback.
